<http://purl.org/nanopub/temp/np1/assertion> {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/flytrap-snap-geometry> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/flytrap-snap-geometry> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Venus flytrap snaps when a geometric parameter exceeds a threshold" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/flytrap-snap-geometry> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "A doubly curved leaf with a slowly changing natural curvature snaps between two stable shapes only if the dimensionless parameter combining its size, thickness and curvature is large enough; below the threshold it deforms smoothly, and the snap time is set by the leaf's elastic and poroelastic time scales rather than by cellular motor speed." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/flytrap-snap-geometry> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/TestableHypothesis> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/flytrap-snap-geometry> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "\\alpha = L^4 \\kappa^2 / h^2 \\gtrsim \\alpha_c \\approx 0.8 \\text{ for bistability (snap-buckling); } \\tau_{snap} \\sim \\tau_{poroelastic} = \\mu L^2 / (k E) \\text{ when } \\alpha \\gg \\alpha_c" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/flytrap-snap-geometry> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Assumptions: thin elastic shell of size L, thickness h and mean curvature kappa; curvature change driven by turgor; the leaf is modelled as a shallow shell with one natural curvature slowly varied. Formal status: partial. The existence of a bistability threshold in alpha is established by shallow-shell analysis and numerics (Forterre et al. 2005 report alpha_c about 0.8 and measured alpha from 0.3 to 1.9 across leaves), but the threshold value is not a closed-form theorem and the snap dynamics rest on scaling arguments confirmed by experiment." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/flytrap-snap-geometry> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/proposedTest> "Measure L, h and the open-state mean curvature of many Dionaea leaves, compute alpha, and test that leaves with alpha below about 0.8 close smoothly while those above snap, as high-speed video classifies them; also check that closure time scales with L^2 for the snapping leaves." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/flytrap-snap-geometry> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hypothesisType> "model-theorem" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/flytrap-snap-geometry> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Yoël Forterre, Jan M. Skotheim, Jacques Dumais & L. Mahadevan (2005) How the Venus flytrap snaps" .
}
